  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 258 PM EDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nCurrent-Tuesday... Surface high pressure remains in place over the \neastern U.S. early this week, gradually broadening on Tuesday. A \ntight pressure gradient is maintained locally across east central \nFlorida as low pressure develops along a stalled frontal boundary \nnear the Bahamas and across the western Atlantic. Gusty northeast \nwinds have continued today, with peak gusts observed around 25-30 \nmph across the interior and higher gusts observed along the coast. \nModels have indicated an additional surge of gusts late this \nafternoon or evening over the Atlantic waters and near the coast, \nand today's peak wind gusts may not be observed until late in the \nday. Therefore, have opted to extend the current Wind Advisory along \nthe coast through tonight, ending at 8AM Monday, and cannot rule \nout additional extensions further through Monday. With elevated \nonshore winds forecast to persist the next several days, numerous \ncoastal hazards will continue. Life threatening rip currents and \nrough surf are present at all local beaches, and a High Surf \nAdvisory is in effect for large breaking waves 7-12 ft in the surf\nzone. Entering the water is not advised! Pounding surf and long \nperiod onshore swells will continue Moderate to Major beach \nerosion at times of high tide. Of note, this significant coastal \nevent coincides with a period of astronomical high tides, and \nMinor to Moderate saltwater flooding is also forecast. During this\nmorning's high tide cycle, the observation gauge at Trident Pier \ncrested at 2.84 ft (MHHW Datum), reaching Moderate Flood Stage. \nAdditional higher crests are expected during the Monday and \nTuesday morning high tide cycles, and saltwater flooding will \nimpact low-lying roads, lots, docks, and yards near the coast and \nintracoastal waterways. A Coastal Flood Warning remains in effect.\n\nShifting focus towards the precipitation forecast and trends, a \npocket of higher moisture (PWAT ~ 1.7-1.9\") across portions of the \nTreasure coast and near Lake Okeechobee fueled several rounds of \nonshore moving showers across the south early today. Highest \nrainfall totals from showers that occurred early this morning were\ngenerally observed around Fort Pierce (~2-2.5\") and Port St. \nLucie (~1.5\"). While coverage further north has been more limited\nup to this point, an additional plume of moisture should increase\ncoverage of onshore moving showers north of the Cape this \nafternoon and early this evening with some activity sneaking \ninland. More periodic rounds of isolated to scattered showers may \ncontinue overnight and on Monday, especially near the coast. A \ndrier airmass will then work to reduce overall rain chances by \nTuesday. Temperatures aloft (500mb) have warmed several degrees \ncompared to yesterday, and poor lapse rates above 700mb will \nlargely limit lightning potential.\n\nAfternoon temperatures remain seasonal, mostly in the low 80s. \nLow temperatures in the mid to upper 60s across the far interior \nwill remain warmer in the low 70s near and east of the Orlando \nMetro and Lake Okeechobee\n\nWednesday-Saturday... High pressure across the eastern U.S. becomes \nreinforced by a second area of high pressure moving near the Great \nLakes mid week. Breezy and gusty onshore flow looks to persist each \nday through the back half of the week, and poor to hazardous coastal \nconditions should generally continue alongside a period of high \nastronomical tides. Models show differences in moisture through \nthe extended period, but generally expect rounds of scattered \nonshore moving showers to return with the highest coverage along \nthe coast and across inland areas south of the Cape. Near to \nslightly above seasonal temperatures are forecast with highs in \nthe low to mid 80s.",
